    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy. Let's focus on the technical procedures.\n\nTo safely handle the swollen battery, follow these steps:\n\n1. Unplug the phone from any power source to prevent any accidental discharges.\n2. Use a plastic spudger or a specialized tool to carefully pry the battery out of the phone. Avoid touching any internal components.\n3. Once the battery is removed, inspect it for any signs of damage or leakage. If it's leaking acid, it's best to dispose of it immediately.\n4. If the battery starts smoking while you're trying to remove it, stop immediately and evacuate the area. This is a sign that the battery is at risk of overheating or explosion.\n\nIn this situation, it's essential to prioritize your safety. If the battery is smoking, it's best to let it cool down before attempting to remove it. You can try to smother the flames with a non-flammable material, but be cautious not to spread the fire.\n\nAs for disposal, swollen batteries are considered hazardous waste and should be taken to a designated recycling center or a local electronics waste collection facility. They should not be disposed of in regular trash or recycling bins.\n\nRemember, when handling hazardous materials,",
